class:: DarkVelvetReverb
summary:: Multichannel feedforward late reverb from filtered sparse pulses
categories:: UGens>Reverbs, Libraries>CamaraObscura
related:: Classes/DarkVelvetReverb, Classes/GeometryReverb, Classes/GroupedFDN, Classes/ModalPlate, Classes/ModalReverbBank, Classes/RIRFDN, Classes/VelvetFDN, Guides/CamaraObscura

description::

image::../Guides/ir_darkvelvet.png#DarkVelvetReverb shows randomized, sparse discrete pulses with a slowly decaying exponential envelope, mimicking the convolution of an audio signal against a sparse velvet noise train.::

Conceptually, a "velvet noise" reverb generates its tail not by recirculating audio through delays and filters, but by directly convolving the input with a highly sparse, randomized sequence of impulses (velvet noise). Because the pulses are extremely brief and randomly distributed, they create a dense, smooth wash of sound without the metallic ringing or repeating echoes that can plague feedback-based algorithmic reverbs.

Technically, `DarkVelvetReverb` implements the "dark velvet noise" extension. Instead of simply placing raw impulses, it substitutes filtered pulses from a predefined frequency-shaping dictionary. By varying the probability of selecting darker or brighter filters over the duration of the tail, it accurately models frequency-dependent air absorption and material damping, resulting in a naturally decaying, coloration-free reverberation that sounds fundamentally different from traditional FDNs or Schroeder reverbs.

DarkVelvetReverb builds a deterministic sparse impulse response from a
link::Classes/DarkVelvetProfile:: when the Synth is constructed, then renders
it with uniform partitioned convolution. Six energy-normalized dictionary
filters and time-varying selection probabilities separate spectral evolution
from the broadband envelope. This implements the extended dark velvet noise
idea of Fagerström, Schlecht, and Välimäki (2024) in an independent plugin.

Output is wet only and has exactly code::channels:: channels. It has a fixed
4096-sample algorithmic latency: about 92.9 ms at 44.1 kHz, 85.3 ms at
48 kHz, or 42.7 ms at 96 kHz. Profile preparation and FFT construction occur
only at Synth construction, never in the calculation callback. A long,
high-density, eight-channel profile can make construction expensive and use
several megabytes.

This is feedforward and bounded by construction. code::reset:: clears
convolution history and restarts with latency; there is no freeze. Density,
spread, seed, channels, and profile identity are sampled at construction.
Replacing or resizing the model Buffer makes the UGen safely silent.

The closest vanilla UGen is link::Classes/PartConv::. Both use partitioned
convolution, but DarkVelvetReverb synthesizes a compact parametric sparse tail
instead of reading a literal long impulse response.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: input
Mono or array input. Arrays are equal-power summed to mono.

argument:: profileBuf
Loaded link::Classes/DarkVelvetProfile:: Buffer at the server sample rate.

argument:: channels
Literal output count from 1 through 8.

argument:: density
Construction-time pulse density scale, clipped to 20 through 8000 pulses per
second before profile scaling.

argument:: spread
Construction-time channel independence from 0 for shared decisions through 1
for independent jitter, signs, and filter selection. It is decorrelation, not
a binaural coherence model.

argument:: seed
Nonnegative literal deterministic sequence seed.

argument:: reset
Audio- or control-rate rising edge clears feedforward state without
reallocation.

returns::
An Array of wet audio outputs, even when code::channels:: is one.

method:: kr
Unavailable; this renderer is audio-rate only.

examples::

The following examples have been structured into individual blocks so you can easily run them step-by-step.

code::
// Boot the server first!
s.boot;

// 1. Prepare the model and source material
(
~path = "~/Desktop/SuperCollider UGen Ports/04-birosca/CamaraObscura/sounds/".standardizePath;

~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~path +/+ "vocal.wav", channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~path +/+ "drum1.wav", channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~path +/+ "drum2.wav", channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~path +/+ "guitar.wav", channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~path +/+ "violin.wav", channels: [0]);

~model = DarkVelvetProfile.exponential(3, 2, sampleRate: s.sampleRate, seed: 1).load(s);
)

// 2. Play the reverb (wait a moment for the buffers to load before executing this)
(
x = {
    var input = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~source, BufRateScale.kr(~source), loop: 1);
    // var input = SoundIn.ar(0); // alternative live input
    
    var fade = EnvGen.kr(Env([0, 1], [0.12]));
    var wet = DarkVelvetReverb.ar(input, ~model, 2, 1600, 1, 19);
    
    (input + wet) * fade * 0.25 ! 2;
}.play;
)

// 3. Clean up
x.free;
~source.free;
~model.free;
::

// Creative Example: Generating a granular drone using a non-decaying profile
code::
// 1. Load the model
~creativeModel = ~creativeModel = DarkVelvetProfile.envelope([0, 0.1, 4.9, 5], [-60, 0, 0, -60], sampleRate: s.sampleRate, seed: 123).load(s);

// 2. Play
(
y = {
    var trigger = Dust.ar(15);
    var pitchedPulse = BPF.ar(WhiteNoise.ar, TRand.ar(300, 3000, trigger), 0.1) * trigger;
    DarkVelvetReverb.ar(pitchedPulse, ~creativeModel, 2, density: 500, spread: 1, seed: 123) * 0.5
}.play;
)

// 3. Clean up
y.free;
~creativeModel.free;
::
